  "interpretation": "# What the numbers suggest\n\n## What changed\n\nOver 90 days, 3,490 people used the tool for 1,586,890 actions. Weekly users fell from 830 in mid-August to 323 in late September, then climbed to 1,093 in the week of October 25. The last 14 days had 1,621 people versus 638 before, up 154.08%. Actions rose 148.8%, so each person did about as much as before. Estimated work rose only 55.13%, which may mean newer people start with lighter tasks.\n\nUpgrades move fast. Release 2.6.7 reached 32.39% of people in its first, partial week.\n\n## How people use it\n\nOf everyone, 59.6% came back. Among 2,937 new people in the last 30 days, 50.6% returned after a week and 37.01% after three weeks. Working with cells and tables made up 65.2% of actions. \"Share of work\" is an estimate from fixed effort levels: quick reads count 1, everyday edits count 3 and heavy data work counts 10. It is not time or speed. By that estimate, macros are 7.2% of actions but 14.99% of work, and the data import tool is 3.56% versus 9.55%. Advanced use starts early: 78.13% of data import users and 77.37% of macro users tried it on their first day.\n\nAI assistant sessions had a middle size of 11 actions. Sessions with 201+ actions were 4.46% of sessions but held 44.79% of actions. Of sessions, 70.32% used more than one area, most often cells with sheets. Typical waits were 1.18 seconds for data imports and 0.1 seconds for cell work; waits depend on workbook size and computer, not quality. Workdays averaged 36,165 actions, weekends 17,646. Since September 28, the 396 command line users, a different group, averaged 1,099.8 actions each versus 234.4 for 3,849 assistant users. Over this shorter window, command line use seems more focused on cells, at 81.96% of actions versus 59.69%.\n\n## Where people get it\n\nDownloads count downloads, not people. One person can download several times, updates and automated installs add to totals, and channels overlap, so totals should not be added. npm totals cover 12 months: 10,837 for the AI assistant package and 5,498 for the command line. Since each channel started, release files reached 30,386 and editor extension installs 9,677. Weekly npm downloads peaked at 5,178 in the week of October 4, then 2,176 by October 18. Weekly gains in other channels grew from 1,794 to 3,285, mostly release files. Release 2.5.7 led recent releases with 509 downloads, then 2.6.1 with 244.\n\n## What we will improve\n\nThese are plans, not findings. We will make cell and table work smoother, since it is most of what people do. We will help newcomers reach a second week, and add guidance for long sessions and for macros and data imports, which carry much of the estimated work.",
